# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; this file is not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Dev server
PORT=5000
BASE_PATH=/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file mixes TWO runtimes. Read the labels:
#
#   1. SERVER-ONLY secrets (HUNYUAN_*, OPENAI_*) — read by the Pages Function
#      and the Vite dev proxy. NEVER prefix with VITE_. In production set them
#      in the Cloudflare dashboard as Secrets; for `wrangler pages dev` put
#      them in .dev.vars (see .dev.vars.example).
#
#   2. CLIENT-SAFE config (VITE_*) — inlined into the public browser bundle.
#
# If a value would be catastrophic to publish, it belongs in group 1, never
# behind a VITE_ prefix.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# === DEFAULT tier: the dedicated OCR model (Hunyuan) ===
# Runs on every page. There is no default key and no fallback: with this unset,
# /api/ocr returns 503 rather than quietly using a committed credential.
HUNYUAN_API_KEY=

# Base URL and model for the Hunyuan endpoint.
HUNYUAN_BASE_URL=https://api.futureppo.top/v1
HUNYUAN_MODEL=hunyuan-ocr

# === Administrators ===
# Comma-separated, matched EXACTLY by server/admin.ts. Server-only: this decides
# access, so it must never be VITE_-prefixed. Unset falls back to the owner
# address built into server/admin.ts. See .dev.vars.example for the full note.
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# === ESCALATION tier: OpenAI ===
# Runs ONLY to re-extract a page the default tier read with low confidence
# (below the account's confidence threshold). Its daily allowance is far
# smaller than the default tier's, so it must never become the default path —
# RULES.ocrEscalation in server/rate-limit.ts caps it per account per day.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Base URL and model for the OpenAI endpoint.
# NOTE: the .dev.vars example sets OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.4-mini — keep the two
# consistent when copying values across.
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.4

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLIENT-SAFE config.
#
# WARNING: anything prefixed with VITE_ is inlined into the JavaScript bundle
# and is publicly readable by anyone who opens DevTools. There are no VITE_
# OCR keys: extraction goes through /api/ocr, which holds the credential
# server-side. Never add a browser-side provider key.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Google Identity Services client ID (public by design).
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# VITE_APP_API_BASE_URL and VITE_UPLOAD_API_URL used to be listed here and are
# gone: nothing reads them. src/lib/api-paths.ts resolves every call against
# import.meta.env.BASE_URL and appends /api, so the API is always same-origin --
# which is what lets the session cookie be HttpOnly + SameSite=Lax at all. A
# configurable API origin would need CORS and a cross-site cookie, so it is a
# deliberate absence rather than a missing feature.

# EmailJS is NOT configured here, and cannot be.
#
# It used to be, as VITE_EMAILJS_* -- which Vite inlines into the published
# bundle. Service id + template id + public key is the full argument set for the
# EmailJS send endpoint, so "public by design" was wrong: it is a
# send-on-our-behalf credential.
#
# Two things now enforce that rather than describing it. The server reads only the
# unprefixed names (functions/api/auth/password-reset.ts), and
# forbidSecretViteVars() in vite.config.ts fails the build if VITE_EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY
# has a value -- it used to be allowlisted there, which meant the guard waved
# through the one name it would otherwise have caught.
#
# See .dev.vars.example for the server-side names.
